{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '241,520p' TRANSLATE_PLAN.md\"","aggregated_output":"  decision itself stays human.\n\n### AI agent selection\n\nAI-driven scripts invoke a shared runner at `scripts/agent_runner.py`.\nClaude is the default provider for compatibility with the original\ntranslation run, but each driver can switch to Codex with\n`--agent codex` or `XLATE_AGENT=codex`.  Common model overrides are\n`--model`, `XLATE_AGENT_MODEL`, `XLATE_CLAUDE_MODEL`, and\n`XLATE_CODEX_MODEL`.\n\nClaude receives the script's `--allowedTools` list directly.  Codex has\nno equivalent per-run allowlist flag, so the runner appends the intended\ntool scope to the prompt and invokes `codex exec` with\n`--sandbox workspace-write --ask-for-approval never` by default\n(overridable with `--codex-sandbox` / `--codex-approval`).\nProvider transcripts are separated under `xlate/claude_logs/` and\n`xlate/codex_logs/`.\n\n## Phase 1A — AI self-review of translated headers\n\nAfter Phase 1 lands a Rust stub for each header, run a self-review\npass: an AI re-reads each translated module against the project's\nquality bar (safety, consistency, idiomatic Rust) and applies\nimprovements in place.\n\nThis is *not* a regeneration.  The existing translation is the\nstarting point and represents real work that already passes the\ngate.  The reviewer Edits, never Writes from scratch.\n\n### Top-level rule: idiomatic Rust, not C with Rust syntax\n\n**The translation should not look obviously C-derived.**  The\nguiding question is: would a Rust programmer who hadn't seen the\nC source write this?  If not, change it.  Specifically:\n\n* **Naming convention is universal.**  `quic_t` → `Quic`.\n  `cnx_t` → `Cnx`.  `state_enum` → `State`.\n  `pmtud_policy_enum` → `PmtudPolicy`.  Drop the `_t` and `_enum`\n  suffixes; PascalCase types and traits; PascalCase enum variants;\n  snake_case fields and methods; `SCREAMING_SNAKE` constants.\n  This applies to **every** name — types, traits, fields, enum\n  variants, free functions — not just traits.\n* **Free functions on a primary `&T` / `&mut T` argument are\n  methods on `T`.**  `set_low_memory_mode(quic: &mut Quic, …)`\n  → `impl Quic { fn set_low_memory_mode(&mut self, …) }`.  Same\n  for getters, builders, and any function whose first argument\n  is the type's \"self\".\n* **Use Rust's memory model.**  `Drop` replaces explicit `free`\n  (`fn free(self: Box<Quic>)` → `impl Drop for Quic`); `Vec` /\n  `Box` / owning `String` replace `malloc`/`free` pairs;\n  `Rc<RefCell<_>>` only when ownership genuinely is a graph;\n  no `Box<T>` parameters when the function doesn't need\n  heap-stable storage (clippy's `boxed_local` flags these).\n* **Use Rust's error model.**  `Result<T, Error>`, not\n  `Result<T, ()>` or `i32` status codes.  Sentinel return values\n  like `-1` map to `Err`, not `Ok(-1)`.\n\n### What else to look for\n\n* **Safety holes** — raw pointers used without a clear `// SAFETY:`\n  story, `unsafe` blocks that have a safe equivalent, ownership\n  patterns that smell wrong.\n* **Type-shape consistency** — the same C type translated\n  differently in different functions of the same module;\n  signed/unsigned choices that disagree with caller arithmetic;\n  integer widths that drift from the C source.\n* **Idiom plumbing** — `&[T]` vs raw pointer + length,\n  `Option<&T>` for nullable, owned `String` vs borrowed `&str`\n  for caller-supplied text, function-pointer typedef → trait,\n  doc-comment placement.\n* **Lint allowances** — `#![allow(…)]` blocks added to silence\n  the initial gate are usually a smell.  Tighten to the minimum\n  scope, or fix the underlying code instead of suppressing.\n* **Documentation** — every public item should have a doc comment\n  explaining non-obvious shape decisions.  A note like\n  `C: \\`picoquic_create\\`` is fine for traceability but is not\n  a substitute for explaining what the Rust function does.\n\n### Per-header procedure\n\n1. Read `TRANSLATE_PLAN.md` (this document) and `CLAUDE.md`.\n2. Read the C header, the matching `.c` file, and the existing\n   Rust translation at `rs/fq/src/<path>.rs`.\n3. Identify improvements.\n4. Apply them via `Edit` operations.  No full rewrites.\n5. Run `cargo check` and `cargo clippy -- -D warnings` from\n   `rs/fq/`.  Iterate until both pass.\n6. Report the changes in a one-paragraph summary on stdout.\n\nIf nothing needs changing, say so on stdout and exit without\nediting — that's a valid outcome.\n\n### Scripting\n\n`scripts/phase1a.py` drives the pass:\n\n* Iterates Phase 1 `ok` headers in topological order.\n* Per header: composes a review prompt, invokes the configured agent\n  with `Read Edit Glob Grep Bash(cargo check) Bash(cargo clippy)` as\n  the intended tool scope — no `Write`, this is refinement only.\n* Tracks state in `xlate/phase1a_state.json` (status: `ok` /\n  `fail` / `noop`).\n* Resumable; continues on failure by default.\n* Per-header transcripts at `xlate/<agent>_logs/phase1a/<path>.log`.\n\n## Phase 1B — Cross-module consistency report\n\n1A is per-header by design — one agent invocation sees one module\ndeeply but isn't well-suited to spot patterns that vary *between* modules\n(trait naming conventions, lint allowances, type definitions\nthat drift, the same C type translated differently in different\nplaces).  A whole-crate AI pass would burn context and turns\nwithout a clear consistency oracle to compare against.\n\nPhase 1B is **pure inspection — no AI, no edits**.  A script\nscans `rs/fq/src/` and emits a markdown report cataloging the\npatterns that vary across modules.  The human reviewer reads\nthe report, decides on consistency policies, and then sprinkles\n`// REVIEW: <instruction>` markers in the source for Phase 1C\nto address.\n\n### What the report contains\n\n* **Trait names by case style** — snake_case (mirroring C\n  typedefs) vs. PascalCase (Rust idiom), with module locations\n  for each.\n* **Module-level lint allowances still present** — which `#![allow]`\n  attributes survive in which modules.  Diversity here suggests\n  inconsistent reasoning during translation.\n* **Type definitions across modules** — every `pub struct`,\n  `pub enum`, `pub type` declaration, with duplicates flagged\n  (same name defined in multiple modules: usually means an\n  opaque stub somewhere needs to be replaced by the real\n  definition's import).\n* **Cross-module imports** — `use crate::picoquic::…` lines\n  with the imported items grouped by source module.\n\n### Scripting\n\n`scripts/phase1b.py` is a single-shot inspection — no agent,\nno state machine.  It writes `xlate/consistency_report.md` and\nexits.\n\n```sh\npython3 scripts/phase1b.py             # write the report\npython3 scripts/phase1b.py --json      # also dump raw data\n```\n\n### Output goes back into 1C\n\nThe human reviewer reads `xlate/consistency_report.md`, decides\nthe policy (\"all traits PascalCase except those whose typedef\nname is part of a callback ABI\" or whatever), and adds\n`// REVIEW: <instruction>` markers where the source disagrees\nwith the policy.  Phase 1C then implements the changes.\n\n## Phase 1C — Human review with AI execution\n\nAfter 1A and 1B, the human reviewer reads each module and the\nconsistency report, then annotates the code with\n`// REVIEW: <instruction>` comments wherever they want changes.\nComments can name a target type, a desired pattern, a question\nto investigate — anything actionable.  Running\n`scripts/phase1c.py` then asks the AI to address them.\n\n### How `// REVIEW` comments work\n\n* Plain `// REVIEW: <instruction>` — the AI addresses the request\n  and removes the comment.\n* `// REVIEW(open): <reason>` — the AI tried and could not resolve\n  automatically; the comment stays for further human attention.\n\nThe plain form is greppable as `// REVIEW: ` (with the colon and\nspace) so the open form doesn't trigger another pass.\n\n### Per-file procedure\n\nFor each file containing `// REVIEW: ` markers:\n\n1. Read the file and the inline REVIEW comments.\n2. For each comment, attempt the requested change.  When done,\n   remove the `// REVIEW: ` line.  When stuck — the request needs\n   more context than the file gives, requires breaking the gate,\n   or the human's intent is unclear — rewrite as\n   `// REVIEW(open): <reason>` instead.\n3. Run `cargo check` and `cargo clippy -- -D warnings`.  Iterate.\n4. Report a one-line summary: how many resolved, how many left\n   open.\n\n### Scripting\n\n`scripts/phase1c.py` drives the pass:\n\n* Scans `rs/fq/src/` for files containing `// REVIEW: ` (the\n  open form is excluded).\n* For each, invokes the configured agent with the same intended tool\n  scope as 1A and a prompt naming the file plus the extracted REVIEW\n  comments (line numbers + text).\n* Tracks state in `xlate/phase1c_state.json`, keyed by Rust\n  file path.\n* Supports `--file`, `--list`, `--limit`, `--dry-run`,\n  `--stop-on-failure`, `--force`.\n\n### Iteration\n\n1B and 1C can interleave: the human reads the report, adds\n`// REVIEW:` markers, runs 1C, regenerates the report, repeats.\nEach 1C iteration is cheap because `phase1c.py` only touches\nfiles with fresh REVIEW markers — already-resolved files are\nskipped.\n\n### Phase 1 acceptance gate (revised)\n\nPhase 1 is complete when:\n\n1. Every in-scope header has a Rust module under `rs/fq/src/`.\n2. `cargo fmt` + `cargo clippy -- -D warnings` + `cargo check`\n   all pass.\n3. Phase 1A self-review has run on every module.\n4. The Phase 1B consistency report has been generated and\n   reviewed; any policy decisions have been applied via 1C.\n5. No plain `// REVIEW: ` comments remain (only `// REVIEW(open):`,\n   each with a human-actionable reason recorded).\n\n## Phase 2 — Dependency abstraction\n\nPhase 1 stubs the *library's* surface; Phase 2 stubs the surface\nbetween the library and everything outside it.  The Rust port has\n**no fixed external dependencies** — every capability the library\nborrows from the outside world is reached through a trait.  A\nconcrete implementation (picotls, OpenSSL, the OS sockets layer)\nsatisfies that trait, but is interchangeable.  This is what lets v2\nswap picotls for rustls, swap OpenSSL for `ring`, or run on an\nembedded target with no allocator changes.\n\nThis phase runs after the per-module Phase 1 work has settled\n(1A → 1B → 1C) and before Phase 3 brings tests into the picture —\ntests need a concrete dependency wiring to link, and that wiring\nshould be the abstraction layer's first consumer, not an ad-hoc\nshim.\n\nPhase 2 splits into three sub-phases that mirror the 1A/1B/1C\nshape: an AI-drafted plan (2A), a human-reviewed plan with\niterative revision (2B), and an AI-driven implementation that\nfollows the agreed plan (2C).  The plan is the artifact carried\nbetween sub-phases — it lives at `xlate/phase2_plan.md` and is the\nsingle source of truth for what gets abstracted, how, and behind\nwhich Cargo features.\n\n### Top-level rules (apply to all of 2A/2B/2C)\n\n* **Two-direction traits.**  For each capability boundary, define\n  *both* directions explicitly:\n  * **Provider trait** — calls that flow *from* the library *into*\n    the dependency.  The dependency implements this trait.\n    Naming convention: capability noun, no suffix (`TlsStack`,\n    `AeadCipher`, `RandomSource`, `Clock`).  These traits live\n    next to the module that consumes them.\n  * **Callback trait** — calls that flow *from* the dependency\n    back *into* the library.  The library implements this trait\n    (or supplies a struct that does).  Naming convention:\n    capability noun + role (`TlsCallbacks`, `PacketSink`).  These\n    traits also live next to the consuming module, even though\n    the *implementation* is on a library type.\n* **No concrete dependency types in the library's public API.**\n  No `picotls::ptls_t` in a function signature, no `openssl::EVP_*`\n  in a struct field.  If the C source exposed one, the Rust port\n  hides it behind the relevant trait and the trait's associated\n  types.\n* **Prefer `core` / `alloc` / `std` over external dependencies.**\n  Where the C source borrows from a library only because C lacks\n  the facility (file I/O, string formatting, integer parsing, byte\n  buffer manipulation, sorted maps), the Rust port uses the\n  standard library directly:\n  * `FILE*` + `fprintf` → `core::fmt::Write` for formatting,\n    `std::io::Write` (gated on the `std` feature) for byte sinks.\n  * `printf`-style formatting → `write!` / `format!`.\n  * `qsort` → `slice::sort_by`.\n  * `memcpy` / `memmove` → slice assignment / `copy_from_slice`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' TRANSLATE.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Translating a C project to Rust\n\nThis is a general methodology for translating a C library or binary to Rust.\nThe Rust binary should provide the same functionality as the C code (including\nbug-for-bug compatibility), and its structure and performance should be as\nsimilar as possible without sacrificing safety.\n\nThe overall approach is to move from the \"outside in\".  Start by translating any\ninterfaces that are exposed either publicly or to tests.  Then translate the\nimplementation of the interface methods, the methods that these methods call,\nand so on down the call tree.\n\n> **Review note (overall):**\n> The \"outside-in\" framing is sound for *interface shape*, but for the\n> *implementation* it inverts the natural data flow.  In Phase 3 the plan\n> already pivots to a bottom-up order (lowest-height first), which is the\n> right call — leaf functions have no `todo!()` dependencies, can be unit-\n> tested in isolation, and don't churn when callers are rewritten.  Worth\n> stating explicitly up front that \"outside-in\" governs *API design* while\n> \"bottom-up\" governs *implementation order*.\n>\n> RLB: This makes sense.  Feel free to amend the above to reflect that\n> combination.\n>\n> CLAUDE: Will do — I'll fold \"outside-in for API, bottom-up for\n> implementation\" into the intro on the next edit pass.\n>\n> Major risks not addressed by the plan as written:\n>   - **Shared mutable state.**  Idiomatic C uses pointers into a global or\n>     parent struct; idiomatic Rust uses ownership trees + borrows.  A purely\n>     mechanical port will fight the borrow checker constantly.  Plan needs\n>     an escape hatch (e.g., \"fall back to `Rc<RefCell<…>>` or arena indices\n>     when ownership is genuinely a graph\") and a policy for when to refactor\n>     vs. when to preserve C structure.\n>\n>    RLB: Yeah, we can have Rc<RefCell<_>> as an escape hatch.  Feel free to\n>    indicate that in the appropriate places.\n>\n>    CLAUDE: Acknowledged.  I'll add it to the Phase 1 pointer-mapping rules\n>    and to the cross-cutting policy section as the last-resort option.\n>\n>   - **Bug-for-bug compatibility vs. safety.**  These goals conflict at the\n>     edges: integer overflow, signed/unsigned conversion, UB on aliasing,\n>     uninitialized reads.  Pick a tie-breaker rule.\n>\n>     RLB: The tie-breaker is safety.  The safe option always wins.\n>\n>     CLAUDE: Got it.  Practical implication: when the C code relies on\n>     wrap-on-overflow, signed-shift-as-arithmetic, or memcpy of overlapping\n>     buffers, we use the safe Rust idiom (`wrapping_add` only if the\n>     wraparound is intended; `Result` on out-of-range cast; `copy_from_slice`\n>     with non-overlapping slices) even if outputs diverge for pathological\n>     inputs.  I'll capture that as an explicit rule.\n>\n>   - **Differential testing.**  The strongest correctness signal for a port\n>     is running C and Rust side-by-side on the same inputs and diffing\n>     outputs.  The plan should reserve a phase for this; unit tests alone\n>     will miss endianness, padding, and FP-rounding divergences.\n>\n>     RLB: A separate phase isn't needed.  The translated tests suffice.\n>\n>     CLAUDE: Fine.  Tradeoff worth noting: ported tests inherit the C tests'\n>     blind spots — anything the C suite never exercised (e.g., a specific\n>     overflow path) we won't catch either.  If a downstream user reports\n>     \"tests pass but bytes differ from the C version,\" that's the signal to\n>     add differential testing as a follow-on phase.\n>\n>   - **Cutover strategy.**  Nothing in the plan describes how the Rust port\n>     replaces the C build, nor whether intermediate states ship.  Decide\n>     early: hard fork, or incremental FFI shim where Rust modules are\n>     callable from the C build (and vice versa) until parity.\n>\n>     RLB: There is no cut-over strategy, this is a hard fork.\n>\n>     CLAUDE: Clean.  That eliminates a lot of complexity (no FFI shim, no\n>     mixed builds, no ABI-stability requirement on the Rust side).\n\n## Phase 0: Inventory\n\n* Analyze the build system to extract information needed by later phases\n\n> **Review note (Phase 0):**\n> *Feasibility:* High, but this phase is dramatically under-specified given\n> how much of the plan depends on it.  Concretely, Phase 0 should produce:\n>   - the full source file list (with public vs. internal classification),\n>   - per-file compile flags and `-D` defines (these change the AST),\n>   - the include graph,\n>   - the link graph (which symbols are public ABI vs. internal),\n>   - the test inventory (binaries, fixtures, expected outputs),\n>   - the third-party dependency list with version pins,\n>   - platform conditionals (`#ifdef _WIN32`, etc.) — decide which targets\n>     the Rust port supports on day one.\n>\n> *Pitfalls:*\n>   - Build systems often have multiple entry points (CMake + Makefile +\n>     IDE projects); pick one as canonical and ignore the others, or you\n>     will translate dead code paths.\n>\n>   RLB: I'm willing to assume that this is a CMake project and focus on the\n>   CMake build.\n>\n>   CLAUDE: Good simplification — CMake is the canonical build, everything\n>   else (Makefile, IDE projects, appveyor.yml, etc.) is ignored.\n>\n>   - Generated source (lex/yacc, protobuf, codegen scripts) is easy to\n>     miss and breaks downstream phases.\n>   - Conditional compilation: `#ifdef` branches that are dead on your\n>     target platform should be pruned before parsing, or stubs will\n>     proliferate.\n>\n>   RLB: I am worried about this.  Any thoughts on how to address?\n>\n>   CLAUDE: A few options, in increasing rigor:\n>     1. **Run the preprocessor first.** CMake's `compile_commands.json`\n>        already pins the right `-D` flags for the chosen target.  Pipe each\n>        translation unit through `clang -E -P` (or `unifdef -D… -U…`) to\n>        materialize one fully-expanded source per file with all `#ifdef`s\n>        resolved.  libclang then parses an unambiguous AST.  This is the\n>        default I'd reach for.\n>     2. **Pin a single target triple.** Pick one (e.g.,\n>        `x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu`) as the source of truth.  Code that's\n>        purely `#ifdef _WIN32` simply isn't translated in v1; other\n>        platforms get added back later behind Rust `#[cfg(target_os = …)]`.\n>     3. **Inventory pruning.** Before parsing, run a script that lists\n>        every `#ifdef` symbol used in the codebase and classify each as\n>        always-on, always-off, or per-target.  Produces a written manifest\n>        for review.\n>   I'd combine (1) and (2): preprocess for the chosen target, accept that\n>   we lose the other-platform branches, port them in a follow-on if needed.\n>\n> *Scripting:*\n>   - Drive a *real* build with `bear` (or `compiledb`) to capture\n>     `compile_commands.json` — this is the ground truth for files,\n>     flags, and defines.  Don't reverse-engineer CMake by hand.\n>\n>   RLB: I'm willing to assume that the C project has a CMake build and focus on\n>   that.  So we would use `cmake` to get the `compile_commands.json`.  That\n>   should be useful.  My only concern is that that only addresses the host\n>   platform.  How can we address other platforms?  Maybe with\n>   cross-compilation?\n>\n>   CLAUDE: Yes, cross-compilation is the standard answer.  `cmake\n>   -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=…` per target produces one `compile_commands.json`\n>   per platform; you get an inventory per target and can union the symbol\n>   set or diff between them.  But I'd defer this.  Pick one target now, get\n>   the single-target port working, then add others as a follow-on.\n>   Multi-platform parsing roughly doubles the inventory complexity for a\n>   benefit you only collect at the end of v1.  Concretely: target\n>   `x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu` first; everything else gets a v2 task.\n>\n>   - Use `clang -MM` (or `clang-scan-deps`) for the include graph and\n>     `nm`/`objdump` on built `.o` files for the symbol/link graph.\n>   - Parse with libclang (Python bindings) to dump function signatures,\n>     struct layouts, and call edges to JSON.  Every later phase consumes\n>     this JSON.\n>   - Treat the Phase 0 output as a versioned artifact (`inventory.json`)\n>     checked into the repo so re-runs are diffable.\n>\n>   RLB: I would add to this list making a script we can use to visualize\n>   progress along the dependency graph, possibly at the level of files.\n>\n>   CLAUDE: Yes — build it as part of Phase 0's deliverable.  Inputs: the\n>   libclang call/include graph (already produced) plus a scan of the Rust\n>   crate for `todo!()` markers.  Output: a single static HTML page\n>   (Graphviz SVG, or interactive via pyvis/d3) with nodes colored\n>   C-only / stubbed / translated / translated-and-tested, plus a per-file\n>   rollup percentage.  Default to file-granularity rendering with\n>   click-to-expand to function granularity — the full function graph is\n>   unreadable as one image.  Per-test sub-graphs (the call tree below one\n>   test) are also useful since that's the unit Phase 3 operates on.\n\n## Phase 1: Create a stub implementation\n\nFor each header:\n* Translate to safe, idiomatic Rust module.  Add an empty `mod test` at the end.\n* todo!() implementation\n* Primitive, enum, struct values should translate directly\n* For pointer fields\n    * read C functions that reference the field to understand life cycle\n    * Map to appropriate Rust semantic — slice, vector, linked list, borrow, str, etc.  \n* For pointer args\n    * Read the function\n    * Map to appropriate Rust semantic — ref, mut ref, out param, etc. \n* Do not translate anything external.  These should be stdlib or crates, or worst case sys \n* Function pointers should be mapped to traits\n* Function pointers should be mapped to traits\n\nAfter translating each file, format the crate and verify that crate builds and\npasses `cargo clippy`.  If there are any errors or warnings, fix them.\n\n> **Review note (Phase 1):**\n> *Feasibility:* This is the highest-judgment phase and the *least*\n> automatable part of the plan.  Expect it to dominate the schedule.\n> Mapping a C pointer to the right Rust shape (`&T`, `&mut T`, `Box<T>`,\n> `Rc<RefCell<T>>`, `Vec<T>`, `&[T]`, arena index, raw pointer) requires\n> reading every caller and understanding lifetimes — that is exactly the\n> kind of cross-cutting reasoning that resists scripting.\n>\n> *Pitfalls:*\n>   - **Stub signatures lock in API decisions.**  A wrong choice here\n>     (e.g., `&T` where the real semantic is shared mutation) cascades\n>     into Phase 3 rewrites.  Treat early signatures as drafts and\n>     budget revisits.\n>   - **Dependency cycles between modules.**  C headers freely include\n>     each other; Rust modules form a DAG.  Mutual recursion across\n>     module boundaries forces either merging modules or hoisting types\n>     to a common parent.  Decide the policy before you start.\n>\n>   RLB: This is not a problem.  Modules can cross-depend on each other. For\n>   example, the following code builds just fine.\n> \n>   ```\n>   mod a {\n>       pub fn x() { super::b::y(); }\n>       pub fn z() {}\n>   }\n>   \n>   mod b {\n>       pub fn y() { super::a::z(); }\n>   }\n>   ```\n>\n>   CLAUDE: You're right at the function level — `super::` paths resolve\n>   fine and `rustc` happily handles mutual function references across\n>   modules.  The remaining wrinkle is *type* recursion: `struct A { b: B }`\n>   and `struct B { a: A }` won't compile without an indirection (`Box<B>`,\n>   `&'a B`, or hoisting both into a shared parent module).  Worth a\n>   one-liner in the policy: types that mutually reference get an indirection\n>   (`Box`/`Rc`) or are co-located.  Function-level cycles are a non-issue.\n>\n>   - **Macros and X-macros.**  Constants behind `#define`, function-like\n>     macros, and table-generation X-macros need a per-case decision:\n>     `const`, `fn`, `macro_rules!`, or `build.rs` codegen.\n>\n>   RLB: Agreed.  The tool can use judgement here.\n>\n>   CLAUDE: Acknowledged.\n>\n>   - **Bitfields, packed structs, unions, flexible array members.**\n>     These do not have direct safe-Rust equivalents.  Either accept\n>     `repr(C)` + unsafe accessors, or translate to a different shape\n>     and accept layout divergence.  Pick a rule.\n>\n>   RLB: I don't think this is quite accurate.  Maintaining memory\n>   representation isn't required, just semantic equivalence.  Bitfields and\n>   packed structs can be semantically represented in safe Rust.  Unions should\n>   likewise map to Rust enums.  Flexible array members should map to boxed\n>   slices.\n>\n>   CLAUDE: You're right, I overstated the constraint.  Concrete mappings:\n>   bitfields → ordinary integer fields with mask/shift accessor methods (or\n>   `bitflags!` for flag-style bitsets); packed structs → normal structs\n>   plus serialization helpers for any wire-format use; unions → `enum`;\n>   flexible array members → `Box<[T]>` or `Vec<T>`.  The only exception is\n>   structs that cross an FFI/syscall boundary where layout is part of the\n>   contract — those keep `#[repr(C)]`.  I'll fold this into the Phase 1\n>   rules.\n>\n>   - **`todo!()` everywhere makes the crate technically compile but\n>     hides type errors that only surface when bodies are filled in.**\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"The plan material confirms the Rust port should stay under `rs/fq/`, preserve source-level behavior, and use tests as the gate. I’m doing one more bounded read of the remaining plan/discussion sections, then I’ll start with `qlog_frame.rs`."}}
